  </div>
</section>

{# When to Clean - Seasonal Calendar #}
<section class="service-explanation">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="explanation-grid">
      <div class="explanation-image">
        <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dxzw1zwez/image/upload/gutter-cleaning-process_placeholder.webp" alt="Professional gutter cleaning in progress" loading="lazy">
      </div>
      <div class="explanation-content">
        <p class="section-eyebrow">Timing Matters</p>
        <h2>When to Clean Your Gutters in North Alabama</h2>
        
        <p>Timing your gutter cleanings right makes all the difference. Clean too early in fall and the leaves haven't dropped yet. Wait too long and you're dealing with frozen, compacted debris in January. After 20+ years of gutter work in the Valley, here's the schedule that works:</p>
        
        <p><strong>Late March / Early April:</strong> After pollen season winds down and before spring storms hit. This clears out winter debris and any early seed drop from trees.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Late May:</strong> Oak trees release those "helicopter" seeds (samaras) that pile up fast. A quick cleaning now prevents the spring storm season from turning minor debris into major clogs.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Late October:</strong> First wave of leaf fall. Most deciduous trees around Huntsville drop leaves starting mid-October. Getting ahead of this prevents the worst buildup.</p>
        
        <p><strong>Late November / Early December:</strong> Final cleanup after leaves are done falling. This is the most important cleaning of the year—you want gutters clear before any winter freezes.</p>
        
        <p><strong>After Major Storms:</strong> If a spring storm drops branches on your roof, it's worth checking your gutters. Storm debris can cause clogs that lead to overflow during the next rain.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# Pine Needle Problem #}
<section class="why-local">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="why-local-grid">
      <div class="why-local-content">
        <p class="section-eyebrow">The Pine Needle Problem</p>
        <h2>Why North Alabama Homes Need Extra Attention</h2>
        
        <p>If you've got Loblolly pines anywhere near your house—and most of us in the Huntsville metro do—you already know the drill. Those needles pile up on your roof and slide right into your gutters. By October, you've got a brown, soggy mat that blocks everything.</p>
        
        <p>Pine needles are worse than leaves for two reasons. First, they slip through most gutter guards. The needles are thin enough to get past screens and mesh that would stop oak leaves. Second, they don't just sit there—they mat together, hold moisture, and decompose into acidic sludge that can accelerate rust and corrosion.</p>
        
        <p>The heaviest pine needle drop in North Alabama happens September through early November. If you've got pines within 50 feet of your roofline, you probably need three cleanings a year instead of two: one in late spring, one in early fall after the needle drop, and one in late fall after the leaves.</p>
        
        <p>We see the pine needle problem most often in Hampton Cove, Madison, Harvest, and the Toney area—anywhere with those pine stands that were left when subdivisions went in. If you're in one of these areas and your gutters clog faster than expected, the pines are usually why.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="why-local-image">
        <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dxzw1zwez/image/upload/pine-needles-gutter_placeholder.webp" alt="Pine needles accumulating in gutter" loading="lazy">
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

