{# DIY vs Professional #}
<section class="comparison-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <p class="section-eyebrow">The Honest Truth</p>
      <h2>Can I Clean My Gutters Myself?</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div class="pricing-content">
      <p>Yes, you can—and plenty of homeowners do. If you're comfortable on a ladder, have a single-story home, and don't mind heights, DIY gutter cleaning is straightforward. You'll need a sturdy ladder, gloves, a scoop or trowel, a bucket, and a garden hose.</p>
      
      <p>That said, here's the reality:</p>
      
      <p><strong>Safety is a real concern.</strong> Ladder falls cause over 500,000 injuries per year in the U.S., and gutter cleaning is one of the most common activities when those falls happen. Two-story homes, steep roofs, and soft ground (after rain) increase the risk significantly.</p>
      
      <p><strong>You might miss problems.</strong> We spot issues that homeowners often can't see from the ladder—loose hangers starting to pull away, small cracks at seams, early fascia rot behind the gutter. Catching these early saves money down the road.</p>
      
      <p><strong>It takes longer than you think.</strong> What looks like a quick job from the ground often takes 2-3 hours when you factor in ladder repositioning, climbing up and down, and actually clearing compacted debris.</p>
      
      <p>Our take: if you've got a single-story home and you're comfortable with the work, DIY can make sense. For two-story homes, steep roofs, or if you'd rather spend your Saturday doing something else, professional cleaning is worth the investment.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

